      "lineNbr": 1323,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002022
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-10-15T18:06:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m a little unclear as to why this is an instance variable for a port.  It seems like most usages of it are like a local variable (guaranteed write before read within a function), and cases where state is preserved (like this one) seem a bit strange.\n\nFor instance, the cases above don\u0027t handle what happens when the Rd in an Rd+Ra drops away and you\u0027re left with an NC+Ra.  If Rd dropped away while transitioning into this state, you could be stuck here forever, because new_cc_state\u003d\u003d-1.  Similarly, if CC initially misdetected as Ra+Ra then debounced to NC+Ra, you would resolve as PD_CC_AUDIO_ACC, which is clearly wrong.\n\nOne could say this is just a bug (the spec says you should be doing the Rd || Rd and then Ra \u0026\u0026 Ra before checking NC _||_ NC), but that would have been more obvious had new_cc_state not been an instance variable to begin with.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1323,
        "startChar": 26,
        "endLine": 1323,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "20706cc975503e6680919af5bef17f86a65cd829",
